;; day8/re-frame2-freehand — the Freehand view substrate artefact
;; (EP-0036; D001 fixes the product name and `re-frame.freehand` as the
;; single public door, conventionally aliased `v`).
;;
;; F1a (rf2-drpa3.15): artefact shape + cross-artefact classpath wiring
;; only — pure build plumbing, no contract content. The descriptor
;; and `v/defview` land with F1b; the interpreted React and
;; JVM trees, `v/mount` and HMR identity with the rest of the F1 spine;
;; the absorbed analyzer and both emitters with F3.
;;
;; The entry namespace is `.cljc` FROM THE OUTSET because the F1 spine
;; needs both emitters: a JVM tree that runs on the JVM (and feeds the
;; ssr artefact) and a React tree in CLJS. There is no later moment at
;; which this artefact becomes cross-host — it starts that way.
;;
;; NO dependency on ../ui, now or ever. EP-0036 §Product topology: the
;; donor artefact is "donor only", and the new package never depends on
;; the donor. Absorbed donor code arrives by MOVE, slice by slice, under
;; Freehand ownership — never by classpath reference. That boundary is
;; gated, not merely stated: the `freehand` workflow greps this artefact
;; for any donor spelling, so even a prose mention here would go red.
;;
;; NO :clein deploy aliases — deliberate, not an omission. Publication is
;; F6 territory, and the donor artefact was itself ruled NOT to be
;; published (2026-07-22), so there is no donor descriptor to mirror.
;; The version-lockstep gate (.github/scripts/verify-version-lockstep.sh,
;; via implementation/scripts/lib/publishable-runtimes.cjs) inventories
;; exactly those artefacts carrying a real `:aliases/:clein/build` key,
;; so staying out of it leaves the release train untouched until Freehand
;; actually ships.
{:paths ["src"]
 :deps  {day8/re-frame2 {:local/root "../core"}}

 :aliases
 ;; TEST-ONLY routing dependency, and deliberately only test-only. The
 ;; `v/route-link` descriptor reaches routing through core's late-bind
 ;; registry (`freehand -> core late-bind <- routing`), so the production
 ;; :paths above stay routing-free; the suites need the real routing
 ;; artefact loaded so its `:routing/link-model` hook is published and the
 ;; anchor is proved against the SHIPPING link law rather than a stub.
 ;;
 ;; day8/re-frame2-spec-resource is the build-time reader the conformance
 ;; fixture loader reads through (`re-frame.freehand.conformance`, under
 ;; test/). Test-only for the same reason the loader is: fixtures are read
 ;; at macro-expansion time, and nothing under :paths above reads a file.
 {:test {:extra-paths ["test"]
         :extra-deps  {day8/re-frame2-routing       {:local/root "../routing"}
                       day8/re-frame2-spec-resource {:local/root "../spec-resource"}
                       ;; TEST-ONLY, and only test-only. The `v/render-static`
                       ;; server-render suite exercises the JVM tree -> HTML seam
                       ;; end-to-end, which folds the structural tree through
                       ;; `re-frame.ssr/emit-ui-tree`. The PRODUCTION :paths above
                       ;; take NO require on re-frame.ssr (the Independence wall —
                       ;; the render reaches the serialiser by LATE resolution at
                       ;; render time), so the artefact is loaded only here so the
                       ;; suite can prove the end-to-end render on one classpath.
                       ;; day8/re-frame2-ssr depends on core alone (never the
                       ;; donor), so this pulls no donor namespace onto the
                       ;; classpath.
                       day8/re-frame2-ssr           {:local/root "../ssr"}
                       day8/re-frame2-test-quiet    {:local/root "../test-quiet"}}
         ;; `-e :prod-gate` excludes the ONE namespace that is a statement
         ;; about the JVM it runs in rather than about Freehand:
         ;; `re-frame.freehand.prod-gate-lane-pin-test` asserts
         ;; unconditionally that `-Dre-frame.debug=false` is on this JVM's
         ;; command line. It belongs to the `:prod-gate` lane below and is
         ;; red anywhere else BY DESIGN — a pin softened to "check the
         ;; property if it is set" would pass vacuously in exactly the case
         ;; it exists to catch (rf2-8alkj). So `:test` and `:prod-gate` are
         ;; a two-way partition; every test still runs exactly once across
         ;; them.
         :main-opts   ["-m" "re-frame.test-quiet.runner" "-e" ":prod-gate"]}

  ;; rf2-8alkj — the PRODUCTION-GATE lane, as a MODIFIER composed onto
  ;; `:test`: `clojure -M:test:prod-gate`. `:extra-paths` / `:extra-deps`
  ;; merge in from `:test` (so the classpath cannot drift between the two
  ;; lanes), while `:jvm-opts` and `:main-opts` come from here —
  ;; `:main-opts` is last-wins in tools.deps, which is what lets this alias
  ;; drop `-e :prod-gate` and pick the pin up.
  ;;
  ;; WHY THE FLAG LIVES IN `:jvm-opts` AND NOT ON A COMMAND LINE. SECURITY.md
  ;; documents `-Dre-frame.debug=false` as the JVM/SSR production setting, and
  ;; `re-frame.interop/debug-enabled?` reads it ONCE at namespace-load time.
  ;; rf2-f8x2i / rf2-hnrwo closed this hole for core, routing and ssr; this
  ;; artefact was the one whole artefact still executed by NO suite under the
  ;; documented production posture. Every suite that calls itself a
  ;; "production gate" test rebinds the Var with `with-redefs` AFTER the
  ;; framework has loaded, and a load-time gate is invisible to that — the
  ;; hole rf2-9c2jf came through in core (`dispatch-sync` a total no-op under
  ;; the gate, green for as long as it existed). Putting the property in the
  ;; alias makes it part of the LANE's definition rather than something a job
  ;; has to remember, and `re-frame.freehand.prod-gate-lane-pin-test` fails
  ;; the run if it ever stops arriving.
  ;;
  ;; WHY FREEHAND SPECIFICALLY. The `{:reactive false}` opt-out (rf2-oxlpy) is
  ;; precisely a SAFETY MECHANISM whose production behaviour is the thing under
  ;; test, and the evidence/elision spine (`re-frame.freehand.evidence`) exists
  ;; to be COMPILED AWAY in a release build. PR #7177 closed the CLJS half of
  ;; that gap; this alias is the JVM half.
  ;;
  ;; NOT THE WHOLE SUITE, YET. Under the real gate a documented slice of this
  ;; artefact's test namespaces is red, essentially all of it asserting dev
  ;; instrumentation (evidence records, `:rf.freehand/*` traces, source coords)
  ;; inline with semantics. Those are legitimate dev-posture tests, not
  ;; defects. The runnable roster lives in
  ;; `scripts/test-freehand-prod-gate.sh`, which appends `-n` selectors for
  ;; every namespace MINUS a documented known-red list, so a NEW namespace
  ;; joins the lane by default. Do not invoke this alias bare; run the script.
  :prod-gate
  {:jvm-opts  ["-Dre-frame.debug=false"]
   :main-opts ["-m" "re-frame.test-quiet.runner"]}

  ;; The B-spine measurement harness (D021). No extra paths and no extra
  ;; deps: the harness is ordinary artefact source under :paths, because
  ;; B5 measures PRODUCTION bundles and a measurement spine that could
  ;; not be compiled into one would measure the wrong artefact.
  ;;
  ;;   clojure -M:bench            the standing evidence suite
  ;;   clojure -M:bench --prove    the two-way falsifiability proof
  ;;
  ;; Stdout is valid EDN (the summary rides as `;;` comments), so a
  ;; release run is `clojure -M:bench > out/freehand-bench.edn`. Exit 1
  ;; means a deterministic property was violated, and nothing else — no
  ;; wall-clock or byte distribution can reach that exit code.
  :bench {:main-opts ["-m" "re-frame.freehand.bench.runner"]}}}
